<!--
    angr test_decompiling_tee_O2_tail_jumps :: setlocale_null_androidfix
    (binaries/tests/x86_64/decompiler/tee_O2, x86-64, GCC -O2).

    At -O2 a leaf function whose last act is "call X; return" is compiled to a
    direct TAIL JUMP: `jmp X` instead of `call X; ret`.  When X is an external
    symbol the jump targets the PLT thunk:

        setlocale_null_androidfix:
            xor    %esi,%esi          ; arg2 = NULL
            jmp    setlocale@plt       ; tail jump (direct BRANCH) to the thunk

    kuna's flow follower runs with the whole address space in-bounds, so it
    treats the direct `jmp setlocale@plt` as ordinary intraprocedural flow and
    follows it INTO the PLT thunk.  The thunk's body is `jmp qword [rip+GOT]`
    (an indirect jump); jump-table recovery fails on it, so it is rewritten to a
    CALLIND through the GOT pointer + a "// jump-as-call" warning.
    The thunk got INLINED instead of the `jmp` being recognized as a tail call,
    so the callee renders as `(*dat_...)(...)` rather than the named `setlocale`.
    angr instead renders `return setlocale(v1, NULL)`.

    Stage model: S2 "flow-classification" sub-stage.  The P0 assertion `option
    tailcalljump on|off` toggles recovering a direct `jmp` to another known
    function's entry as a tail call (BRANCH -> CALL + RETURN).  DIV-11 default-ON;
    when it fires it logs a `// tail-call` WARNING comment.

    This is a minimal reconstruction of the construct: `setlocale_null_androidfix`
    at 0x1000 tail-jumps to the `setlocale@plt` thunk at 0x1040 (mapped/named
    `setlocale`, as PLT-reloc analysis would), whose body indirect-jumps through
    the GOT slot at 0x1080 (unnamed -> dat_1080).

    Pass 1 (option tailcalljump off): assertions #1/#2 prove the bug - the
    "// jump-as-call" recovery-failure marker and the unresolved
    GOT-pointer indirect call `(*dat_1080)`.
    Pass 2 (option tailcalljump on): assertion #3 proves the fix - the tail call
    resolves to the named `setlocale(...)`; assertion #4 proves the logging contract
    - the recovered call is logged (`// tail-call`) (and #1/#2
    stay at exactly one, i.e. the bug is gone in pass 2).
-->
